-- title:
--   Abelian subvariety generated by a set of endomorphisms
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field, $J$ a scheme, $f : J \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ a morphism and $L$ a relative group law for $f$, that is, a functorial group structure (multiplication, unit, inverse, associativity, unit laws, left inverse, and compatibility with base change $T' \to T$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$) on the sets $\{\varphi : T \to J \mid \varphi \circ f = t\}$ of points of $J$ over $k$-schemes $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} k$. Assume $f$ is smooth and proper with connected fibres over every point of $\operatorname{Spec} k$ and admits a relative group law, and that $L$ is commutative on $T$-points for all $T$. Let $S$ be a set of morphisms $\mathcal{T} : J \to J$ with $\mathcal{T} \circ f = f$, each of which is a homomorphism, in the sense that post-composition with $\mathcal{T}$ carries $L$-products of $T$-points to $L$-products. Then there exist a scheme $B$, a morphism $g : B \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, a relative group law $LB$ for $g$ and a morphism $i : B \to J$ with $i \circ g = f$ such that: $g$ is smooth and proper with connected fibres and admits a relative group law; $LB$ is commutative; $i$ is a closed immersion; post-composition with $i$ carries $LB$-products to $L$-products; and for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ with a $k$-algebra structure, an $\Omega$-point $z$ of $J$ factors as $b$ followed by $i$ for some $\Omega$-point $b$ of $B$ if and only if $z$ lies in the subgroup of the group of $\Omega$-points of $J$ (for the group structure given by $L$) generated by the points $x$ followed by $\mathcal{T}$, with $\mathcal{T} \in S$ and $x$ an $\Omega$-point of $J$.
--
--   This is the construction of the abelian subvariety of an abelian variety over a field generated by the images of a family of endomorphisms, obtained as the scheme-theoretic image of a sum map from a finite power of $J$ and identified on geometric points with the subgroup generated by those images. It is used in the analysis of the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the modular curve, where a closed abelian subvariety cut out by Hecke-type correspondences is required.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_GoodReductionJacobian_RelativeGroupLaw_exists_isClosedImmersion_range_eq_closure_endomorphisms_of_field.lean

